1
00:00:00,310 --> 00:00:01,620
A leaf with no owner!

2
00:00:02,010 --> 00:00:04,260
Finally, a case for Detective Basil.

3
00:00:04,650 --> 00:00:06,340
I'm an expert at detecting.

4
00:00:06,499 --> 00:00:08,189
But WHY is it on our desk?

5
00:00:08,470 --> 00:00:10,119
It isn't Chilli's leaf.

6
00:00:11,289 --> 00:00:13,499
Fallen leaf detected.

7
00:00:13,729 --> 00:00:15,259
Colour: green.

8
00:00:15,599 --> 00:00:16,999
Size: big.

9
00:00:17,339 --> 00:00:19,569
Owner: the mango tree.

10
00:00:20,544 --> 00:00:21,235
GLITCH!

11
00:00:21,624 --> 00:00:23,904
Did you tape THAT leaf onto the pandan?

12
00:00:24,378 --> 00:00:25,448
Correct.

13
00:00:25,758 --> 00:00:28,319
Lost property must be returned.

14
00:00:28,628 --> 00:00:29,948
Efficiently.

15
00:00:30,298 --> 00:00:31,148
Hold it!

16
00:00:31,438 --> 00:00:35,498
Nobody tapes anything until Detective Basil cracks the case!

17
00:00:35,887 --> 00:00:37,657
The Case of the Mystery Leaf!

18
00:00:38,085 --> 00:00:39,835
Every case needs a witness.

19
00:00:40,115 --> 00:00:42,355
Our old friend the snail saw everything.

20
00:00:42,635 --> 00:00:44,265
It's hiding in this video.

21
00:00:44,515 --> 00:00:45,755
Can you spot it?

22
00:00:46,329 --> 00:00:47,769
The King of the Void Deck.

23
00:00:48,039 --> 00:00:49,539
He still has my crown.

24
00:00:49,885 --> 00:00:51,055
Clue one: the colour.

25
00:00:51,565 --> 00:00:53,505
Let's match its exact green.

26
00:00:53,856 --> 00:00:55,356
Clue two: the size.

27
00:00:55,736 --> 00:00:57,676
Careful scientists measure!

28
00:00:58,029 --> 00:00:59,529
Clue three: read the leaf.

29
00:00:59,839 --> 00:01:01,159
Every part is a clue.

30
00:01:01,499 --> 00:01:02,639
Leaf it to me!

31
00:01:04,017 --> 00:01:05,697
Joke detected.

32
00:01:05,977 --> 00:01:07,737
Laughter: zero.

33
00:01:08,091 --> 00:01:09,701
Which clue cracks the case?

34
00:01:09,991 --> 00:01:12,111
Show me one, two or three fingers!

35
00:01:20,733 --> 00:01:22,043
Team high-five!

36
00:01:23,513 --> 00:01:24,663
Let's test them!

37
00:01:24,953 --> 00:01:26,183
Clue one first.

38
00:01:29,364 --> 00:01:29,854
Green.

39
00:01:30,544 --> 00:01:31,024
Green.

40
00:01:31,714 --> 00:01:32,494
And...

41
00:01:32,794 --> 00:01:33,264
green.

42
00:01:33,954 --> 00:01:35,294
They're ALL green!

43
00:01:36,464 --> 00:01:37,964
Recalculating.

44
00:01:38,244 --> 00:01:41,094
The leaf belongs to all three plants.

45
00:01:42,427 --> 00:01:44,877
Um... a leaf can only come from one plant.

46
00:01:45,230 --> 00:01:49,450
Most leaves are green, so colour alone cannot tell these three apart.

47
00:01:50,188 --> 00:01:52,318
Every suspect has the same alibi.

48
00:01:53,472 --> 00:01:54,562
Clue two.

49
00:01:54,942 --> 00:01:57,812
The mango tree is the biggest plant, so it must have the

50
00:01:57,832 --> 00:01:59,072
biggest leaves.

51
00:01:59,425 --> 00:02:00,465
Agreed.

52
00:02:00,845 --> 00:02:02,945
Big tree, big leaf.

53
00:02:03,299 --> 00:02:04,259
...Oh no.

54
00:02:04,809 --> 00:02:06,439
GLITCH agrees with me.

55
00:02:08,809 --> 00:02:10,008
One is bigger.

56
00:02:10,459 --> 00:02:11,329
One is smaller.

57
00:02:11,349 --> 00:02:11,728
Um...

58
00:02:13,439 --> 00:02:14,739
that doesn't help.

59
00:02:16,913 --> 00:02:17,553
Pip!

60
00:02:17,943 --> 00:02:20,803
The little pandan plant has the longest leaves of all!

61
00:02:21,531 --> 00:02:24,061
Let's look at that again, in slow motion.

62
00:02:26,242 --> 00:02:29,992
Leaves on one plant come in different sizes, and a small plant

63
00:02:30,012 --> 00:02:31,492
can have long leaves.

64
00:02:31,762 --> 00:02:33,471
So size is a weak clue.

65
00:02:34,893 --> 00:02:37,593
Look closer, the way a scientist does.

66
00:02:37,957 --> 00:02:40,187
The flat, wide part is the leaf blade.

67
00:02:40,617 --> 00:02:43,697
It's usually the biggest part of a leaf, and its edge gives

68
00:02:43,717 --> 00:02:45,047
the leaf its shape.

69
00:02:46,146 --> 00:02:48,366
This thin part is the leaf stalk.

70
00:02:48,746 --> 00:02:51,356
The leaf stalk attaches the leaf to the stem.

71
00:02:52,453 --> 00:02:56,183
From the leaf stalk, the main vein runs down the middle, and

72
00:02:56,203 --> 00:02:58,433
it branches into smaller veins.

73
00:02:59,021 --> 00:03:01,001
But WHERE do all the little veins go?

74
00:03:01,391 --> 00:03:02,661
They're everywhere!

75
00:03:03,011 --> 00:03:05,831
Be a drop of water, on your way into a leaf.

76
00:03:06,111 --> 00:03:07,091
Shrink down.

77
00:03:07,371 --> 00:03:08,301
Quietly.

78
00:03:09,665 --> 00:03:11,775
Last time, you rode up a stem.

79
00:03:12,555 --> 00:03:16,745
This time, you slide into the leaf stalk, a narrow green tunnel.

80
00:03:16,894 --> 00:03:20,514
Now you are in the main vein, the widest path, running down

81
00:03:20,534 --> 00:03:21,154
the middle.

82
00:03:21,298 --> 00:03:23,368
Paths branch off on both sides.

83
00:03:24,138 --> 00:03:27,648
You take one, then a smaller one, then a smaller one still.

84
00:03:27,790 --> 00:03:31,580
They split and join, all over the leaf blade, like a net.

85
00:03:33,057 --> 00:03:35,917
So veins that split and join like a net are called net

86
00:03:35,937 --> 00:03:36,897
veins!

87
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:40,620
Suspect one: the pandan plant.

88
00:03:40,840 --> 00:03:44,170
Its veins run side by side, from one end to the other.

89
00:03:44,517 --> 00:03:46,507
Like the lanes on a running track!

90
00:03:46,807 --> 00:03:49,357
Veins like that are called parallel veins.

91
00:03:50,462 --> 00:03:54,482
Our mystery leaf has net veins, so the pandan plant is cleared.

92
00:03:55,072 --> 00:03:57,052
And it smells like pandan cake.

93
00:03:57,272 --> 00:03:58,562
Sorry, pandan.

94
00:03:58,832 --> 00:04:00,192
You're free to go.

95
00:04:00,540 --> 00:04:02,720
Suspect two: the mango tree.

96
00:04:03,110 --> 00:04:05,150
Its leaves have net veins too!

97
00:04:05,496 --> 00:04:06,496
Net veins!

98
00:04:06,746 --> 00:04:08,196
The mango tree did it!

99
00:04:08,457 --> 00:04:09,626
CASE CLOSED!

100
00:04:09,796 --> 00:04:10,886
Mango.

101
00:04:11,186 --> 00:04:13,066
As I calculated.

102
00:04:13,420 --> 00:04:13,940
Um...

103
00:04:14,650 --> 00:04:15,430
Basil?

104
00:04:15,980 --> 00:04:18,070
Two suspects have net veins.

105
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:20,260
And you're agreeing with GLITCH.

106
00:04:21,635 --> 00:04:23,964
Basil is not an expert at waiting.

107
00:04:24,311 --> 00:04:25,071
Case...

108
00:04:25,431 --> 00:04:26,421
not closed.

109
00:04:26,741 --> 00:04:27,951
Good catch, Pip.

110
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:31,580
But WHY does the mango leaf feel smooth all the way round,

111
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:33,190
and ours feels bumpy?

112
00:04:36,360 --> 00:04:38,380
The mango leaf has a straight edge.

113
00:04:38,650 --> 00:04:42,010
The mystery leaf has a jagged edge, like tiny teeth.

114
00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:43,950
The mango tree is cleared.

115
00:04:44,597 --> 00:04:46,337
Suspect three: the hibiscus.

116
00:04:46,567 --> 00:04:49,707
Net veins, a jagged edge, and the same oval shape with a

117
00:04:49,727 --> 00:04:50,887
pointed tip.

118
00:04:53,064 --> 00:04:54,424
Every clue matches.

119
00:04:54,684 --> 00:04:57,054
The hibiscus dropped the mystery leaf!

120
00:04:57,550 --> 00:04:59,030
NOW it's case closed!

121
00:05:00,890 --> 00:05:02,200
Incorrect.

122
00:05:02,460 --> 00:05:04,590
My scan said mango.

123
00:05:04,840 --> 00:05:06,350
Show me the data.

124
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:07,850
Here's the data.

125
00:05:10,421 --> 00:05:12,341
Pandan: parallel veins.

126
00:05:12,591 --> 00:05:14,241
Mango: a straight edge.

127
00:05:14,621 --> 00:05:16,941
Hibiscus: every clue matches.

128
00:05:18,469 --> 00:05:19,139
Error.

129
00:05:19,659 --> 00:05:21,289
Colour: the same.

130
00:05:21,559 --> 00:05:23,149
Size: different.

131
00:05:23,419 --> 00:05:24,199
Owner...

132
00:05:24,769 --> 00:05:26,009
hibiscus.

133
00:05:26,478 --> 00:05:27,308
You rushed.

134
00:05:27,638 --> 00:05:28,678
I rushed too.

135
00:05:29,008 --> 00:05:31,028
A good detective reads every clue.

136
00:05:31,376 --> 00:05:32,436
Updating!

137
00:05:32,806 --> 00:05:36,186
Lesson stored: read a leaf by its parts.

138
00:05:36,426 --> 00:05:39,086
Colour and size: weak clues.

139
00:05:39,845 --> 00:05:42,855
Returning lost property to the hibiscus!

140
00:05:43,325 --> 00:05:44,185
Aiyoh.

141
00:05:44,505 --> 00:05:48,005
Leaf drop already, cannot stick back one lah.

142
00:05:48,345 --> 00:05:50,615
The plant grows new leaves.

143
00:05:50,955 --> 00:05:52,725
Slowly, slowly.

144
00:05:53,189 --> 00:05:54,779
...Removing tape.

145
00:05:57,416 --> 00:05:58,846
Did you spot the snail?

146
00:05:59,116 --> 00:06:01,806
Just now, it was under a hibiscus leaf!

147
00:06:02,152 --> 00:06:06,192
Snails eat leaves, and a snail's tongue has thousands of tiny teeth!

148
00:06:06,544 --> 00:06:09,064
Our witness was sitting on the answer all along.

149
00:06:09,412 --> 00:06:10,692
Tell someone at home!

150
00:06:10,971 --> 00:06:13,371
Now, here are the words that earn marks.

151
00:06:13,428 --> 00:06:14,638
Say it with me!

152
00:06:15,353 --> 00:06:17,843
The main parts of a leaf are the leaf blade, the leaf

153
00:06:17,863 --> 00:06:20,823
stalk, the main vein and the smaller veins.

154
00:06:21,143 --> 00:06:23,353
The leaf stalk attaches the leaf to the stem.

155
00:06:23,373 --> 00:06:27,193
The veins can be arranged in a parallel pattern or a net

156
00:06:27,213 --> 00:06:28,083
pattern.

157
00:06:30,784 --> 00:06:33,064
Fallen leaf detected.

158
00:06:33,416 --> 00:06:34,376
And whose is it?

159
00:06:35,153 --> 00:06:36,503
Net veins.

160
00:06:36,833 --> 00:06:38,343
Jagged edge.

161
00:06:38,633 --> 00:06:39,873
Hibiscus.

162
00:06:40,083 --> 00:06:42,153
And it stays on the ground.

163
00:06:42,543 --> 00:06:45,013
Basil, am I shorter than a pandan leaf?

164
00:06:45,371 --> 00:06:46,491
Case closed.

165
00:06:46,958 --> 00:06:48,178
Picked clue three?

166
00:06:48,468 --> 00:06:50,648
You win a point, and so does Basil!

167
00:06:53,818 --> 00:06:55,068
...Not me.

168
00:06:55,419 --> 00:06:58,369
Next time, a prickly customer at the Help Desk!

169
00:06:58,957 --> 00:07:01,157
Let's read today's key words together!

170
00:07:01,447 --> 00:07:02,207
Ready?

171
00:07:02,494 --> 00:07:03,564
Leaf blade.

172
00:07:05,042 --> 00:07:06,122
Leaf stalk.

173
00:07:07,600 --> 00:07:08,720
Net veins.

174
00:07:10,274 --> 00:07:11,584
Parallel veins.

175
00:07:13,342 --> 00:07:14,282
Well done!

176
00:07:15,264 --> 00:07:16,368
Say it with us!

177
00:07:17,168 --> 00:07:17,918
Ready?

178
00:07:18,041 --> 00:07:18,701
One!

179
00:07:19,041 --> 00:07:19,741
two!

180
00:07:20,046 --> 00:07:21,221
Now you know!

181
00:07:25,631 --> 00:07:26,771
Case closed!

182
00:07:27,677 --> 00:07:28,547
Basil.

183
00:07:28,887 --> 00:07:30,034
The evidence.

184
00:07:30,054 --> 00:07:31,313
...Case reopened.

185
00:07:31,333 --> 00:07:32,463
Take two!

186
00:07:36,351 --> 00:07:38,771
New knowledge acquired.
